# G002 — Calculator

*v2. See `calculator.md` for prose: the resolver IS the parser,
`@calc` names a capability already latent in the resolver,
expressions with agent-resolved terms are inherently concurrent.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Budget Reconciliation Across Departments

[budget-reconciliation
    IN ORDER TO
        gather one term per department concurrently
        let the calculator walk the expression tree, resolving @references
        and deliver margin as the last reduction step

    concurrent [
        @kathryn{report: "Q4 revenue"}        -> @revenue
    |
        @eliana{report: "infrastructure cost"} -> @infra-cost
    |
        @marcus{report: "staffing cost"}       -> @staff-cost
    ]
    join

    @net        <- [calc {expr: "@revenue - @infra-cost - @staff-cost"}]
    @margin-pct <- [calc {expr: "@net / @revenue * 100"}]

    (@kathryn){present: "Margin is " + @margin-pct + "%"}
]

---

## Verb Surface

[calc
    expr            {expr}                    ;; string -> value
    eval            {ast}                      ;; AST -> value
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: references resolved at descent `where` parser = resolver

[calc {expr: @e}]
    IN ORDER TO
        walk @e as an AST, resolving any @reference before applying operators
        because the resolver IS a recursive descent evaluator —
        it already does this for every `@reference{with: @nested}`

    for each subexpression in @e
        where node is an @reference
            -> resolve it first (may invoke another agent)
        where node is a literal
            -> return the literal
        where node is an operator
            -> evaluate operands (in parallel where independent)
               then apply operator
]

The terms can be computed in parallel; the expression evaluation is
the synchronisation point where results combine. G002 is the
identity project — InnateScript recognising itself.

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [calc {expr: "2 + 3 * 4"}]        ;; -> 14 (precedence respected)
    -> [calc {expr: "(2 + 3) * 4"}]      ;; -> 20
    -> [calc {expr: "10 / 3"}]           ;; -> 3.333...

    @revenue <- 50000
    @costs   <- 12000
    -> [calc {expr: "-(@revenue - @costs)"}]     ;; -> -38000
]
